WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.156 --> 00:00:02.530
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:06.340
I help some nursery experts with
their GCS upload performance.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.440
Will we rock this
problem to sleep?

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.860
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.620
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.430
Bundle Cam is a company that
helps provide peace of mind

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.440
through durable,
high-performance nursery

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:17.980
cameras.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.271
Their set up was pretty simple.

00:00:19.271 --> 00:00:21.340
Every half second, the
camera would snap a photo

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.810
and store it locally.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.580
Later, a batch of
those photos would

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.560
be uploaded to
Google Cloud Storage

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.469
for display and analysis.

00:00:28.469 --> 00:00:30.010
The problem was that
they were seeing

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.110
that their upload times
for a batch of images

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.610
was painfully slow.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.800
During the course of 20
minutes, this slow down

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.300
would result in
a serious backlog

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.760
of images that needed
uploading, eventually resulting

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.790
in the camera running out of
memory and stopping working.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.700
At first glance, this could
be a cloud storage issue.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.370
So let's run down the
list of things to test.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.070
Firstly, we ran perf
diag on a sample bucket

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:54.190
that they created and verified
that it had a high throughput

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.764
from clients in the region.

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:58.180
This meant that the bucket
itself was performing properly,

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.520
in terms of connection and
upload speed from the client,

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.680
so that wasn't our problem.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.459
Next, we checked that they
were using the fastest API.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:07.420
As we we've shown,
the gsutil API

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.680
tends to be a tad slower than
the direct API counterpart,

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.630
but that wasn't
the problem either.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.460
The cameras were on
embedded hardware,

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.320
so they were forced to
use the direct APIs,

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.360
rather than gsutil.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.360
And they were using the
parallel upload API, so again,

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.890
no problem there.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.590
At this point, I needed to
do some research myself.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.520
So I turned to Michael
Yu's 2017 Next Talk

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.390
where he provided details on
how GCS works behind the scenes.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.420
The important bit for us
goes something like this.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.150
When uploading files to
GCS, the load balancer

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.250
will use file
names to decide how

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.050
to distribute connections
between shards

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.280
to handle upload.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.710
As such, the more diverse
the file names, the better

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.450
the system can balance the load.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.820
Likewise, the more
similar the file names,

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.240
the harder it is to
reach peak performance

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.240
when there's contention to GCS,
and that was exactly our issue.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.570
The developer was using the
time stamp in the file path,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.850
running right into this
performance problem.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.190
The fix for Bundle
Cam was pretty simple,

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.770
prepending the hash of the
file name to the file name

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.380
itself, which allowed
GCS to better partition

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:14.547
the fixed range into shards
for better upload performance.

00:02:14.547 --> 00:02:16.630
You can see the difference
between the linear file

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.330
names and the hashed
ones pretty easily.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.120
To learn more about how Bundle
Cam improved their upload

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.070
performance, check out
the related article

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:25.610
linked in the description below.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.735
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our Cloud

00:02:27.735 --> 00:02:29.110
Performance Atlas content.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:30.740
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.110
every millisecond counts.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

